{% doc %}
  Validates only what the caller supplied. Whether the address belongs to a real
  or an unverified account is deliberately NOT validated here - that decision
  lives in create.liquid, which stays silent about it so the endpoint cannot be
  used to test which addresses are registered.

  @param {object} object - The object to process
  @param {object} hcaptcha_params - The hCaptcha parameters
  @param {boolean} verify_hcaptcha - Whether the calling endpoint presented an hCaptcha widget whose answer should be checked
{% enddoc %}
{% liquid
  assign c = { "errors": {}, "valid": true }

  function c = 'modules/core/validations/presence', c: c, object: object, field_name: 'email', key: 'modules/user/validation.email.required'
  function c = 'modules/core/validations/email', c: c, object: object, field_name: 'email', key: 'modules/user/validation.email.format'
  function c = 'modules/core/validations/presence', c: c, object: object, field_name: 'host'

  comment
    The captcha belongs to the endpoint that rendered the widget, not to this
    command: only a caller that showed one can be asked for the answer. Callers
    that did not - registration, which has already validated its own form - pass
    verify_hcaptcha false, and must not reach the filter at all, because the
    hcaptcha filter raises a LiquidArgumentError on a blank or empty argument
    rather than returning false. That error propagated out as a 500 for the whole
    request, which is why this is gated on the caller rather than on whether any
    params happen to be present.
  endcomment
  if verify_hcaptcha and context.constants.VERIFY_HCAPTCHA == "true"
    function c = 'modules/core/validations/hcaptcha', c: c, hcaptcha_params: hcaptcha_params, key: null
  endif

  assign object.valid = c.valid
  assign object.errors = c.errors

  return object
%}
